---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SpivaLab from "../../components/SpivaLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Can Active Managers Beat the Market?"
  description="The SPIVA scorecard, made interactive: pick a fund category and horizon, and see the share of professional active funds that failed to beat their benchmark. The longer the clock runs, the more of them fall behind."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Can active managers beat the market?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Actively managed funds hire full-time professionals to pick winners and beat
      the market. So how often does it actually work? Twice a year, S&P Dow Jones
      Indices tallies the answer in its <em>SPIVA</em> scorecard — the share of
      active funds that failed to beat a simple index. Pick a category and a time
      horizon, and watch the pattern that has held for decades.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <SpivaLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why beating the market is so hard</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It's a zero-sum game before costs.</strong> For every investor who
          beats the market, another must lag it — the average dollar earns the market
          return. After fees and trading costs, the average actively managed dollar
          must therefore <em>trail</em> the index. William Sharpe called this "the
          arithmetic of active management," and it's simple math, not opinion.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Time is the enemy of the stock-picker.</strong> A fund can beat the
          market for a year on luck. But as the horizon stretches to 10, 15, or 20
          years, luck washes out and costs compound — which is why the failure rates
          above climb steadily toward the top of the chart.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Yesterday's winners rarely stay winners.</strong> The funds that
          top the charts one period are, on average, no more likely than any other to
          lead the next — so picking them ahead of time is closer to a guess than a
          skill. That's the case for owning the whole market cheaply instead.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this says active management is worthless, or that <em>no one</em> beats
        the market — a few do. It says beating it reliably, after costs, over a long
        horizon is rare and hard to identify in advance. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="active-vs-passive" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/active-vs-passive" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
</style>
